WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.394
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.394 --> 00:00:05.977
protect a shared counter with synchronized

00:00:05.977 --> 00:00:08.437
methods and wait for both workers before

00:00:08.437 --> 00:00:10.036
reporting its final value.

00:00:10.715 --> 00:00:13.615
Synchronization helps applications preserve

00:00:13.615 --> 00:00:16.717
shared inventory and reservation totals across

00:00:16.717 --> 00:00:19.685
overlapping requests, while completion waits

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:22.315
establish when a final report is ready.

00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:25.759
A campus supply desk receives counts from two

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:26.811
preparation jobs.

00:00:26.811 --> 00:00:29.720
The first models one thousand completed checks,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.887
and the second models two thousand.

00:00:31.887 --> 00:00:34.734
Every check should add exactly one to the same

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:35.229
counter.

00:00:35.229 --> 00:00:38.015
These loop iterations model work; they do not

00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:40.677
inspect supplies or measure real processing

00:00:40.677 --> 00:00:41.048
speed.

00:00:41.792 --> 00:00:44.292
We expect a final total of three thousand, with

00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:45.942
every requested check included.

00:00:45.942 --> 00:00:48.123
We'll give both workers the same counter,

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:50.623
protect each increment, start both workers, and

00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:52.538
join both before printing one total.

00:00:52.538 --> 00:00:54.666
Later, you'll predict the total when the

00:00:54.666 --> 00:00:55.943
requested counts change.

00:00:55.943 --> 00:00:58.337
The final number will tell us about preserved

00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:00.891
updates, not which worker ran first or how often

00:01:00.891 --> 00:01:01.742
they alternated.

00:01:02.477 --> 00:01:04.957
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace.

00:01:04.957 --> 00:01:07.764
We'll first define the object that owns the

00:01:07.764 --> 00:01:10.831
shared value, then define the work that updates

00:01:10.831 --> 00:01:11.027
it.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.269
The main method starts the caller's execution.

00:01:18.269 --> 00:01:21.176
It declares InterruptedException because our

00:01:21.176 --> 00:01:23.224
later joins can be interrupted.

00:01:23.224 --> 00:01:26.130
The expected final report follows the normal

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.905
path where both joins finish successfully.

00:01:41.185 --> 00:01:44.179
Let's create SharedCounter with a private total

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:45.454
that begins at zero.

00:01:45.454 --> 00:01:48.257
Private controls direct access to the field.

00:01:48.257 --> 00:01:51.315
It does not, by itself, prevent two workers from

00:01:51.315 --> 00:01:53.035
interfering with an update.

00:02:04.687 --> 00:02:07.451
An increment reads the old total, adds one, and

00:02:07.451 --> 00:02:08.803
writes the replacement.

00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:11.449
If unsafely shared, two executions could read

00:02:11.449 --> 00:02:13.919
the same old value and both write the same

00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:15.683
replacement, losing an update.

00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:17.917
We'll make the entire increment method

00:02:17.917 --> 00:02:20.328
synchronized so cooperating calls on this

00:02:20.328 --> 00:02:22.974
counter cannot perform that transition at the

00:02:22.974 --> 00:02:23.562
same time.

00:02:33.316 --> 00:02:36.070
This instance method uses the counter object's

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.943
intrinsic monitor, its built-in mutual-exclusion

00:02:38.943 --> 00:02:39.542
mechanism.

00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:41.996
A caller must acquire that monitor before

00:02:41.996 --> 00:02:43.972
entering the synchronized method.

00:02:43.972 --> 00:02:46.845
Another synchronized call using this same object

00:02:46.845 --> 00:02:49.539
must wait until the monitor is released; this

00:02:49.539 --> 00:02:52.113
does not promise fair turns or a particular

00:02:52.113 --> 00:02:52.891
worker order.

00:02:52.911 --> 00:02:55.746
Let's make the getter synchronized too, then

00:02:55.746 --> 00:02:57.292
close the counter class.

00:02:57.292 --> 00:03:00.384
Both methods will use the same object's monitor,

00:03:00.384 --> 00:03:03.219
so reads and updates follow one coordination

00:03:03.219 --> 00:03:03.541
rule.

00:03:03.541 --> 00:03:06.569
A synchronized getter alone would not repair an

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:07.986
unprotected increment.

00:03:15.843 --> 00:03:17.718
Now we'll define IncrementJob.

00:03:17.718 --> 00:03:20.468
Each job needs a reference to the counter it

00:03:20.468 --> 00:03:23.281
should update and a count specifying how many

00:03:23.281 --> 00:03:24.655
increments to request.

00:03:24.655 --> 00:03:27.405
The constructor records those two inputs; it

00:03:27.405 --> 00:03:29.468
does not copy the counter object.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:00.888
The private counter field belongs to each job,

00:04:00.888 --> 00:04:03.727
but both fields can refer to the same counter.

00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:06.442
This is shared mutable state: one changeable

00:04:06.442 --> 00:04:08.664
object is reachable by both workers.

00:04:08.664 --> 00:04:11.626
Let's implement Runnable's run method to request

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:13.724
the assigned number of increments.

00:04:39.357 --> 00:04:41.667
Each loop iteration calls increment and

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:44.450
therefore participates in the counter's monitor

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:44.746
rule.

00:04:44.746 --> 00:04:47.411
The protection covers one complete read, add,

00:04:47.411 --> 00:04:49.188
and write transition per call.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:51.853
It does not reserve the counter for an entire

00:04:51.853 --> 00:04:54.281
job's loop, and it does not turn the Java

00:04:54.281 --> 00:04:56.946
expression into a promise about one processor

00:04:56.946 --> 00:04:57.657
instruction.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:00.807
Let's construct exactly one SharedCounter for

00:05:00.807 --> 00:05:01.415
this run.

00:05:01.415 --> 00:05:03.106
Its total begins at zero.

00:05:03.106 --> 00:05:06.218
We will deliberately share this object because

00:05:06.218 --> 00:05:09.465
both preparation jobs contribute to one combined

00:05:09.465 --> 00:05:09.938
result.

00:05:18.793 --> 00:05:20.718
Now let's create two workers.

00:05:20.718 --> 00:05:23.772
The first job requests one thousand increments

00:05:23.772 --> 00:05:26.228
and the second requests two thousand.

00:05:26.228 --> 00:05:29.415
Passing counter to both constructors gives their

00:05:29.415 --> 00:05:32.203
jobs references to the very same protected

00:05:32.203 --> 00:05:32.668
object.

00:05:49.154 --> 00:05:51.988
Constructing a Thread prepares an execution path

00:05:51.988 --> 00:05:53.642
but does not start its work.

00:05:53.642 --> 00:05:56.181
Let's start both workers before waiting for

00:05:56.181 --> 00:05:56.594
either.

00:05:56.594 --> 00:05:59.192
Scheduling may let their calls interleave in

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:01.790
different ways, but each completed protected

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.507
increment must preserve the previous total and

00:06:04.507 --> 00:06:04.979
add one.

00:06:11.734 --> 00:06:14.358
Protection and completion solve different

00:06:14.358 --> 00:06:14.934
problems.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:17.878
Synchronized prevents cooperating updates from

00:06:17.878 --> 00:06:20.887
overlapping inside the protected transition; it

00:06:20.887 --> 00:06:23.959
does not tell the caller that all requested work

00:06:23.959 --> 00:06:24.727
is finished.

00:06:24.727 --> 00:06:27.671
Let's join both started workers before reading

00:06:27.671 --> 00:06:28.759
the final result.

00:06:38.561 --> 00:06:41.258
A normal join establishes that its worker has

00:06:41.258 --> 00:06:43.774
terminated and makes that worker's earlier

00:06:43.774 --> 00:06:45.572
actions visible to the caller.

00:06:45.572 --> 00:06:48.269
Joining only one would not establish that the

00:06:48.269 --> 00:06:49.407
other had finished.

00:06:49.407 --> 00:06:52.224
Now let's read through the synchronized getter,

00:06:52.224 --> 00:06:54.561
print the total, and close the program.

00:07:09.773 --> 00:07:12.638
The counter started at zero, and the two jobs

00:07:12.638 --> 00:07:15.375
request three thousand increments in total.

00:07:15.375 --> 00:07:18.239
Both use the same protected counter, and both

00:07:18.239 --> 00:07:19.831
joins precede the report.

00:07:19.831 --> 00:07:22.823
Let's click Run and compare that reasoning with

00:07:22.823 --> 00:07:23.523
the output.

00:07:33.215 --> 00:07:36.243
Total is three thousand: one thousand plus two

00:07:36.243 --> 00:07:38.679
thousand, with each update preserved.

00:07:38.679 --> 00:07:41.510
The joins establish that both jobs finished

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:42.630
before this read.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.592
The single output line does not reveal worker

00:07:45.592 --> 00:07:48.555
order, fairness, simultaneous execution, or a

00:07:48.555 --> 00:07:49.740
speed improvement.

00:07:49.846 --> 00:07:52.477
Let's change only the requested counts: two

00:07:52.477 --> 00:07:55.230
thousand for the first job and three thousand

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:56.147
for the second.

00:07:56.147 --> 00:07:58.656
The next complete run will create a fresh

00:07:58.656 --> 00:08:01.286
counter starting at zero and fresh workers,

00:08:01.286 --> 00:08:03.856
while keeping the same synchronization and

00:08:03.856 --> 00:08:04.896
completion rules.

00:08:29.028 --> 00:08:31.522
Before running, predict the new output.

00:08:31.522 --> 00:08:34.527
Explain separately why no increment is lost and

00:08:34.527 --> 00:08:37.468
why the caller can treat the reported value as

00:08:37.468 --> 00:08:37.851
final.

00:08:37.851 --> 00:08:40.920
Use the shared object, the protected transition,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.478
and both normal joins in your reasoning.

00:08:51.711 --> 00:08:54.702
Let's click Run to test your prediction with the

00:08:54.702 --> 00:08:55.886
two new job counts.

00:09:05.709 --> 00:09:07.971
Total is now five thousand, matching two

00:09:07.971 --> 00:09:10.686
thousand plus three thousand on a fresh counter.

00:09:10.686 --> 00:09:13.231
Synchronizing the whole update preserves each

00:09:13.231 --> 00:09:15.776
increment, and waiting for both workers makes

00:09:15.776 --> 00:09:17.077
this a completed total.

00:09:17.077 --> 00:09:19.565
One successful run of an unprotected counter

00:09:19.565 --> 00:09:22.223
would not prove that its updates were safe; our

00:09:22.223 --> 00:09:24.259
explanation depends on the program's

00:09:24.259 --> 00:09:25.334
coordination rules.

00:09:25.465 --> 00:09:28.158
You used one shared object, one monitor rule for

00:09:28.158 --> 00:09:30.796
its operations, and two completion waits before

00:09:30.796 --> 00:09:31.357
reporting.

00:09:31.357 --> 00:09:33.489
For a transfer task, describe a shared

00:09:33.489 --> 00:09:36.014
reservation count where checking availability

00:09:36.014 --> 00:09:38.539
and decreasing the remaining amount must stay

00:09:38.539 --> 00:09:39.044
together.

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:41.345
Explain what belongs inside the protected

00:09:41.345 --> 00:09:43.758
transition and why the caller still needs a

00:09:43.758 --> 00:09:45.890
completion rule before a final report.
